<GuidePage
  guideSlug="quilt-seam-allowance"
  guideCategory="reference"
  title="Quilt Seam Allowance: How the ¼-Inch Seam Affects Cut Size"
  description="See how quilting seam allowance changes rectangle, border, and sashing cut dimensions, and how to keep the assumption consistent."
>
  <section>
    <h2>Why a quarter-inch seam matters</h2>
    <p>
      Patchwork commonly uses a ¼-inch seam allowance. Fabric inside the seam is
      not visible in the finished top, so a piece specified by finished size
      needs allowance on every edge that will be sewn. For a basic rectangle,
      add twice the seam allowance to each dimension.
    </p>
    <p>
      With ¼-inch seams, a 4-inch finished square is cut at 4½ inches. A border
      that finishes 3 inches wide is cut 3½ inches wide. Sashing that finishes 2
      inches wide is cut 2½ inches wide. The same rule applies in metric when
      the chosen allowance is entered in millimetres or centimetres.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2>Nominal, scant, and tested seams</h2>
    <p>
      Thread, pressing, and the fold of the fabric can make a sewn unit finish a
      little small even when the needle is nominally ¼ inch from the edge. Some
      quilters compensate with a scant ¼-inch seam. QuiltClarity does not
      silently choose that adjustment: enter the allowance your tested
      construction requires.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>Cut a small test set using the planned dimensions.</li>
      <li>Sew and press it with the project’s thread and pressing method.</li>
      <li>Measure the unit before repeating the cut for the whole quilt.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2>Where to apply it</h2>
    <p>
      Add allowance when converting a finished design measurement into a cut
      measurement. Do not add it again to cutting dimensions supplied by a
      pattern. The{' '}<a href="/guides/finished-vs-cut-size/"
        >finished-vs-cut guide</a
      >{' '}shows that distinction. For project-specific math, use the{' '}<a
        href="/calculators/borders/">border calculator</a
      >{' '}or{' '}<a href="/calculators/sashing/">sashing calculator</a>; both
      expose the seam allowance they use.
    </p>
  </section>
</GuidePage>
